 "what_changed": {
  "fonts": "The pre-design baseline named IBM Plex but shipped no font file, so a reader without it installed read the product in a fallback face — Arial, or Tahoma for Arabic. The runtime self-hosts the six faces the stylesheet declares (EAD-08) and preloads the two a first paint needs in the page's language. That is the whole of the increase: about 200 KB and three requests on a cold page, and nothing on a warm one.",
  "html": "Comparable, and slightly smaller at the top end despite carrying more structure per page.",
  "social_images": "None. A social image is fetched by a platform when a link is shared, never by the page (EAD-09).",
  "logo": "Unchanged, and still the page: the 10,018,273-byte master is 94-97 % of every cold load. This is now measured on the implemented site, which is what EAD-03 was waiting for."
 },
 "findings": [
  "A cold page transfers 9.83-10.19 MB, of which 94-97 % is the canonical logo. Everything else together is 285-656 KB.",
  "A warm page transfers nothing: every file comes from the browser cache.",
  "Opening Search loads the local index once: 1,976,885 bytes, about 321 KB with gzip, and only on that interaction.",
  "The page makes no external request of any kind: no CDN, no web font service, no analytics, no tracker (gate F6-G05)."
 ],
 "uncertainty": "Single run per page on one machine; byte counts are exact for this build and server, request timing is not measured. Real transfer depends on compression, protocol overheads, caching headers and the final design; the modelled gzip figure is an estimate. No figure here may be presented as the operational or carbon footprint of the released site.",
 "not_claimed": [
  "No carbon figure, and no energy estimate: the release host, its grid and the reader's device are all unknown.",
  "No budget: the method sets one only against the deployed site.",
  "No badge, rating, comparison with another site, or 'green' claim anywhere.",
  "Not a measurement of the release host: compression and caching here are a local static server's."
 ],
